      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hvidovre"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name means ‘White Ovre’, named after the white‑chalk church that stood there, contrasting with Rødovre’s red brick.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hvidovre"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Archaeologists have found evidence that people lived in Hvidovre since prehistoric times.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hvidovre"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1929, a 3,500‑year‑old Bronze Age sword was unearthed in Hvidovre, linking the town to Denmark’s ancient past.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hvidovre"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Located about 10 km southwest of Copenhagen’s center, Hvidovre is the city’s second‑largest suburb after Frederiksberg.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hvidovre"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Between 1918 and 1924, 3,226 of the 3,899 lots in Hvidovre were sold, fueling rapid suburban development.",
